"Jazz is a music genre that originated in the African-American communities of New Orleans, Louisiana, in the late 19th and early 20th centuries. But jazz did not begin as a single musical tradition in New Orleans or elsewhere. It is difficult to define, because it includes a wide range of music. Jazz is characterized by swing and blue notes, complex chords, call and response, polyrhythms, and improvisation.": "e757bf1c94d4",
"Jazz is a music genre that originated in the African-American communities of New Orleans, Louisiana, in the late 19th and early 20th centuries. But jazz did not begin as a single musical tradition in New Orleans or elsewhere. It is difficult to define, because it includes a wide range of music. Jazz is characterized by swing and blue notes, complex chords, call and response, polyrhythms, and improvisation. In swing rhythm the pulse is divided unequally. The first note of each pair of eighth notes is lengthened, and the second is shortened. The first note is often understood to be twice as long as the second, a triplet feel, a ratio of 2:1. But in practice the ratio is often much more subtle. In jazz, the swing ratio typically lies somewhere between 1:1 and 3:1, and can vary considerably. It tends to be wider at slower tempos and narrower at faster ones. A typical jazz group has a rhythm section, which provides the underlying rhythm, harmony, and pulse. The standard rhythm section is piano, bass, and drums. A soloist, or a horn section of woodwinds and brass, plays the melody on top. The bass often plays a walking bass line: unsyncopated notes of equal value, usually quarter notes, like the regular steps of someone walking. Improvisation is one of jazz’s defining elements. Jazz improvisation is the spontaneous invention of melodic solo lines or accompaniment parts in a performance. It is usually done over a chord progression, so it is not random. For example, the twelve-bar blues is based on the I, IV, and V chords of a key. And not all jazz is improvised: swing-era big bands relied more on arrangements that were written or memorized, and the soloists improvised within them. Two more ingredients are blue notes and call and response. A blue note is sung or played at a slightly different pitch from standard, typically between a quartertone and a semitone. Call and response is a succession of two distinct phrases that works like a conversation in music: one musician offers a phrase, and a second answers. Syncopation places rhythmic stresses where they would not normally occur. The tune and the band on this page are our own simple model, built to show one idea. Real players vary a great deal, and not all jazz swings: free jazz explored playing without regular meter or beat. Louis Armstrong (1901–1971) and Duke Ellington (1899–1974) are two of its famous figures. Ellington once said of the music, “It’s all music.”": "bd14e2b34a10",
